WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.909
Film Courage: So there is this saying in the
entrepreneurial world that is “Don’t go

00:00:03.909 --> 00:00:04.909
today.”

00:00:04.909 --> 00:00:08.320
And I guess this means try to keep the lights
on for one more day of doing this.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:15.169
So why do you think so many filmmakers don’t
make another film?

00:00:15.169 --> 00:00:20.150
How do they get there first film made and
then go onto their second and then their third

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:21.180
and then their fourth.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:25.720
A lot of them it seems like don’t go past
the first.

00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:26.720
I can understand why.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:31.890
They are tapped out financially, exhausted,
they didn’t see it have the kind of distribution

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:36.852
that they wanted obviously but you can ask
the same why do people [serial entrepreneurs]

00:00:36.852 --> 00:00:40.190
keep starting companies over and over again
and then go onto another one if it doesn’t

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:41.190
work out?

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.970
Jack Perez, Filmmaker: That’s a really good
question.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:45.670
I think it’s different for everybody.

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:52.510
I think there are people that only have one
movie in them.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:01:00.240
Or in the making of the movie they realize
that that was enough, I don’t need to make

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.920
anymore movies or want to make anymore movies.

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:10.930
But I think most people who are genuine filmmakers,
if they make a film regardless of what happens

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:18.310
whether it did well or it never got distribution,
regardless of what happens, you are constantly

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:22.660
searching for the route to make the next movie
whatever it is.

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:29.330
And so why they don’t make a second movie
may just be that they don’t have the financial

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:35.500
means to make it independently, that perhaps
maybe they’ve gone up for jobs that they

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.500
didn’t get.

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:40.680
I mean I’ve been up for millions of jobs
that I didn’t get.

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:45.850
I don’t know, I have a lot of empathy for
filmmakers who don’t get the next movie

00:01:45.850 --> 00:01:47.940
made because I’ve been one of them.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:53.270
Because I just know what that’s like, it’s
a very painful thing to really want to work

00:01:53.270 --> 00:02:01.229
and make something and not have the means
or maybe the psychological, emotional foundation

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:08.179
to carry forward because there’s a lot of
damage that gets done if it doesn’t work

00:02:08.179 --> 00:02:11.370
out and often most of the time it doesn’t.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:17.990
So there has to be something inside of you
that some how pushes you over all of that

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:21.069
damage to make the next thing.

00:02:21.069 --> 00:02:25.260
And I think those of us that are able to do
that are lucky.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.130
But I’m not sure.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:32.360
I just know that there are a lot of people
who want to make movies that don’t get a

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.360
chance.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:38.870
Film Courage: Do you think they feel angry
that no one appreciate their work?

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.569
Or is it just why bother?

00:02:40.569 --> 00:02:42.260
Jack: I think it’s part of it.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:51.810
I think if you put your heart and soul into
something and no one cares, the tendency to

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:57.130
be bitter or disappointed or even broken is
very likely.

00:02:57.130 --> 00:03:03.010
It’s funny, I remember hearing something
about Marlon Brando who certainly had a rich

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:07.090
and varied career and had no reason to complain
but they said there was a dividing line in

00:03:07.090 --> 00:03:08.090
his work.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:12.540
It was everything before ONE-EYED JACKS which
he directed (his only movie he ever directed)

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:13.540
and after ONE-EYED JACKS.

00:03:13.540 --> 00:03:19.080
They said his personality after ONE-EYED JACKS
was much more “I don’t really care.”

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:25.060
Because he talked about how ONE-EYED JACKS
was he put everything into that movie he wanted

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:27.129
that movie which was the one movie he directed.

00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:29.099
He probably wanted to direct more.

00:03:29.099 --> 00:03:30.970
He wanted that to make a difference.

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:32.180
And people at the time were like “Ahhh?

00:03:32.180 --> 00:03:33.630
ONE-EYED JACKS is whatever.”

00:03:33.630 --> 00:03:35.360
And so he was like “Well forget this!

00:03:35.360 --> 00:03:38.150
Forget it, I’m not going to try or do this
again.”

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:42.030
And he only gave this much instead of this
much [motions to smaller effort].

00:03:42.030 --> 00:03:48.230
Whether that is true or not, I think it’s
understandable.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:53.370
You can get despondent and it’s like if
you’ve been in a bad relationship maybe

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.940
if your heart has been broken, you are not
going to be like “Yeah!

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.069
I’m going to fall in love again.”

00:03:57.069 --> 00:04:04.640
You are kind of like “Yeah…maybe I will…”

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:08.700
But I think if you’ve been hurt, you are
going to stop.

00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.780
I know that SEARCH &amp; RESCUE [Jack’s recent
successful crowdfunding campaign on Indiegogo]

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:12.849
has taken many years for me to get to this
point.

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:17.419
Even though I’ve been super lucky to have
been able to work on a lot of different projects

00:04:17.419 --> 00:04:24.789
over the years, I hesitated putting this into
play because I knew I had to do it myself.

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:29.689
That I had to do it in an unorthodox manner
that it wasn’t all something that was going

00:04:29.689 --> 00:04:32.069
to all just be set up.

00:04:32.069 --> 00:04:36.400
I knew I had to do everything and that in
and of itself is like once you start to pile

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:37.410
up.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:43.319
All of the stuff you have to do, that in and
of itself can stop you.

00:04:43.319 --> 00:04:50.300
So yes, I think filmmaking (like any art)
is populated by a lot of sensitive people

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:53.440
because to do anything good you have to have
a degree of sensitivity.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:05:00.879
But that also makes you vulnerable to the…to
what happens in the real world when things

00:05:00.879 --> 00:05:01.960
don’t go your way.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:07.740
And maybe those people feel it a little more
strongly and don’t bounce back as readily,

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:08.740
maybe?

00:05:08.740 --> 00:05:11.960
Film Courage: You talked about dating and
relationships it reminds me of going on a

00:05:11.960 --> 00:05:16.169
date and it’s like yeah I’ll have coffee
with this person and it’s just like whatever.

00:05:16.169 --> 00:05:21.419
And the person that you really like it’s
“I…I can’t…I won’t even be able

00:05:21.419 --> 00:05:23.319
to speak.”

00:05:23.319 --> 00:05:26.389
And those are the projects you want to work
on but you’re afraid.

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:28.779
Jack: Because what if it doesn’t work out?

00:05:28.779 --> 00:05:33.009
It’s almost like “What if the movie breaks
up with me?”

00:05:33.009 --> 00:05:34.439
That’s really what it is.

00:05:34.439 --> 00:05:39.710
It’s like I’m going to put my everything
into this and what if the movie dumps me?

00:05:39.710 --> 00:05:44.139
Which is funny, I never thought about it in
those terms but all you have to do is look

00:05:44.139 --> 00:05:51.659
at Terry Gilliam and he’s said some really
interesting things lately.

00:05:51.659 --> 00:05:57.649
Obviously LOST IN LA MANCHA is the easiest
way of representing the idea of going after

00:05:57.649 --> 00:06:01.490
something with everything that you’ve got
and then just to be smited when everything

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:03.699
goes wrong and the movie doesn’t happen.

00:06:03.699 --> 00:06:08.840
So that experience is something that all filmmakers
who have been doing it for years know that

00:06:08.840 --> 00:06:14.090
they have gotten this close or they’ve (in
this case) were actually shooting [the movie]

00:06:14.090 --> 00:06:15.550
and it fell apart.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:20.349
So the movie can break up with you and that
can be devastating and that can be reason

00:06:20.349 --> 00:06:25.479
enough not to start it, not to get into another
movie relationship.

00:06:25.479 --> 00:06:28.379
Film Courage: Sure or you can make your HEARTS
OF DARKNESS [A FILMMAKERS APOCALYPSE] and

00:06:28.379 --> 00:06:29.379
film it.

00:06:29.379 --> 00:06:30.379
Jack: Right, exactly.

00:06:30.379 --> 00:06:31.379
At least you get something.

00:06:31.379 --> 00:06:35.880
Film Courage: And then you have two things…but
that’s a rare instance.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:37.210
Jack: Right.

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:41.180
I think clearly when you watch HEARTS OF DARKNESS
[Francis Ford Coppola] all he wanted was for

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:44.819
him to fall off a pier and break his leg so
he didn’t have to finish the movie.

00:06:44.819 --> 00:06:48.940
So in that case he wanted out because he thought
he was messing it up so badly.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:56.710
And then it turned out that of course he wasn’t
but when you see a documentary about filmmakers

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:01.809
going through that doubt and those fears,
those are the healthiest documentaries.

00:07:01.809 --> 00:07:05.439
There are not a lot of them but those are
the ones I think are the most beneficial to

00:07:05.439 --> 00:07:12.379
filmmakers and students because you understand
that they are not just professionals and now

00:07:12.379 --> 00:07:15.349
they are there and they couldn’t possibly
have had self-doubt and they couldn’t possibly

00:07:15.349 --> 00:07:16.669
be wracked with anxiety.

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:22.129
It’s like no, it doesn’t matter where
you are in your career you are always (if

00:07:22.129 --> 00:07:24.300
it matters) feeling those feelings.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.190
And sometimes those feelings can be overpowering.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:32.289
You have to be able to harness that and use
that energy in some other way to get it done

00:07:32.289 --> 00:07:38.669
but yes we all feel those pressures and anxieties
no matter where you are, at whatever stage

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:39.679
you are at.

00:07:39.679 --> 00:07:43.039
Film Courage: It’s kind of like Jim Carrey
with the Netflix documentary and forgive me

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:44.240
I’m drawing a blank on the name [JIM &amp; ANDY:
THE GREAT BEYOND - FEATURING A VERY SPECIAL,

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:46.879
CONTRACTUALLY OBLIGATED MENTION OF TONY CLIFTON,
he was willing to show various [emotional]

00:07:46.879 --> 00:07:51.509
things and a lot of people aren’t willing
to go there and I understand why and not everybody

00:07:51.509 --> 00:07:53.111
is at the level that Jim Carrey is at.

00:07:53.111 --> 00:07:56.539
I think once you get to a certain level, then
you can show all of that?

00:07:56.539 --> 00:07:57.889
Jack: That’s true.

00:07:57.889 --> 00:08:05.249
Or people are afraid of exposing that for
fear of losing what you have if people see

00:08:05.249 --> 00:08:13.959
that vulnerability or humanness that some
how contributes to people taking that away

00:08:13.959 --> 00:08:16.050
which you’ve striven for for so many years.

00:08:16.050 --> 00:08:21.580
But yes, I think those are the healthiest
documentaries for any filmmaker to see.

